\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Cox vs KCOR under a synthetic null with increasing frailty heterogeneity. Two cohorts are simulated with identical baseline hazards and no treatment effect \emph  {(null by construction)}; cohorts differ only in enrollment-time gamma frailty variance (\(\mittheta _0\)). Despite the treatment hazard ratio at fixed frailty being 1 by construction, Cox regression produces increasingly non-null hazard ratios as \(\mittheta _0\) increases, reflecting depletion-induced non-proportional hazards. In the same simulations, \(\mathrm {KCOR}(t)\) remains centered near unity with negligible post-normalization slope across \(\mittheta _0\) values, which is consistent with the expected null benchmark under the working model and is interpreted diagnostically rather than as proof that confounding has been removed. (Exact values depend on simulation seed and follow-up horizon.)}}{49}{table.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tbl:cox_bias_demo}{{7}{49}{Cox vs KCOR under a synthetic null with increasing frailty heterogeneity. Two cohorts are simulated with identical baseline hazards and no treatment effect \emph {(null by construction)}; cohorts differ only in enrollment-time gamma frailty variance (\(\theta _0\)). Despite the treatment hazard ratio at fixed frailty being 1 by construction, Cox regression produces increasingly non-null hazard ratios as \(\theta _0\) increases, reflecting depletion-induced non-proportional hazards. In the same simulations, \(\mathrm {KCOR}(t)\) remains centered near unity with negligible post-normalization slope across \(\theta _0\) values, which is consistent with the expected null benchmark under the working model and is interpreted diagnostically rather than as proof that confounding has been removed. (Exact values depend on simulation seed and follow-up horizon.)}{table.7}{}}
